          body:
            "A web page is not a thing that lives on your computer. It is a document assembled on demand, from parts that may come from several different machines on different continents, and thrown away the moment you close the tab. Everything strange about web development — caching, loading spinners, broken images, \"it works on my machine\", layout that shifts while the page loads — falls out of that single fact.\n\nThis lesson takes one click and follows it. You will meet the **URL**, which says what you want and where it lives; **DNS**, which turns a human name into a machine address; **TCP** and **TLS**, which build a reliable, private pipe to that machine; **HTTP**, the language spoken over that pipe; and finally the **browser's rendering engine**, which turns the files it receives into pixels. You will also meet the two things that make the web feel slow or fast: the number of round trips a page needs, and the order in which the browser is allowed to do its work.",
