            "**The internet** is a network of networks: millions of independently owned machines connected by agreed-upon rules for moving bytes between them. It carries email, video calls, game traffic and the web.\n\n**The World Wide Web** is one application that runs on top of the internet. It is a system of interlinked documents, identified by URLs, transferred by HTTP, and usually rendered by a browser. The web is not the internet; it is the most visible thing the internet carries.\n\n**A client** is the program that starts the conversation — your browser. **A server** is a program that waits for conversations and answers them. The same machine can be both, but in one exchange the roles are fixed: the client sends a *request*, the server returns a *response*, and the connection then waits for the next request.\n\n**HTTP** (HyperText Transfer Protocol) is the format those requests and responses use. It is *stateless*: every request is self-contained, and the server is not required to remember the previous one. \"HyperText\" means the documents contain links to other documents, which is what makes them a web rather than a pile of files.",
