            "Before the browser asks anyone for anything, it checks whether it already has the answer. There are several layers, checked roughly in this order:\n\n1. **The browser's own HTTP cache.** If you visited the page five minutes ago and the response said it could be stored, the file is on disk and no network request happens at all.\n2. **The service worker cache** (on sites that register one), which can answer with a stored file, a freshly built page, or nothing — whatever the site's script decides.\n3. **The operating system's DNS cache and the router's DNS cache**, which remember recent name lookups.\n\nThis is why a change you made to your own site sometimes does not appear: you are looking at a cached copy. Opening developer tools with \"Disable cache\" ticked, or a hard reload, forces the browser to ask again. Understanding caching is not an optional extra — a large fraction of real-world front-end bugs are cache bugs.",
