            "The HTML arrives as a stream of bytes, and the browser starts working on it before the download finishes. This is the **critical rendering path**, and understanding its order explains most front-end performance advice:\n\n1. **Bytes → characters → tokens.** The browser decodes the bytes (using the charset it was told) and tokenises them: `<p>` is a start tag, `Hello` is text, `</p>` is an end tag.\n2. **Tokens → nodes → the DOM.** The **Document Object Model** is a tree of objects, one per element, attribute and piece of text. It is not the HTML file; it is a live data structure JavaScript can read and change. When JavaScript adds an element, it is editing this tree.\n3. **CSS is fetched and parsed into the CSSOM.** Stylesheets are **render-blocking**: the browser will not paint anything until the CSS it knows about has arrived and been parsed, because painting without styles would flash an unstyled page. Stylesheets are also **script-blocking** — a `<script>` waiting behind a stylesheet will not run until that stylesheet is ready.\n4. **DOM + CSSOM → the render tree.** Only visible nodes: `display: none` elements and the contents of `<head>` are left out.\n5. **Layout (reflow).** The browser computes the exact position and size of every box, in pixels, for this viewport width.\n6. **Paint.** It fills in colours, text, borders, shadows and images into layers.\n7. **Composite.** The layers are combined and handed to the screen.\n\nJavaScript's place in this is the reason for most of the folklore about script placement. A classic `<script>` tag stops HTML parsing while it downloads and runs, because the script might call `document.write` or read the DOM. So a script in the `<head>` delays everything below it. That is why you will see `defer` and `async`:\n\n- **`async`** — download in parallel, then run as soon as it arrives, whenever that is. Order is not guaranteed. Good for independent analytics scripts.\n- **`defer`** — download in parallel, run after the document has finished parsing, in order. Almost always what you want for application code.\n\nOnce the page has painted, the work is not over. Any change to the DOM or styles triggers **style recalculation**, then possibly **layout**, then **paint**, then **composite** again. Changing `color` only repaints. Changing `width` forces layout of that element *and everything after it*, which is why animating `width` is expensive and animating `transform` (which only composites) is cheap. You will meet this again when the lesson on responsive design discusses layout, and much later when you profile a slow page.",
