          body:
            "You do not have to take any of this on faith. Every browser ships the instruments.\n\nOpen any site, press `F12` (or right-click → Inspect), and choose the **Network** panel. Tick **Disable cache**, then reload with `Ctrl+R` / `Cmd+R`. What you see is the request list, and each row is one HTTP exchange.\n\nRead the first row — the one whose **Type** is `document`. That is your HTML. Click it and look through the tabs:\n\n- **Headers** shows the request line, every request header your browser sent, the status code, and every response header. Look for `Content-Type`, `Cache-Control` and `Content-Encoding`. Look for `Set-Cookie` if the site logs you in.\n- **Payload** shows the query string parameters, parsed into a readable table.\n- **Response** shows the raw HTML the server returned — which is often much less than you expect on a JavaScript-heavy site.\n- **Timing** breaks the request into *Queueing*, *DNS lookup*, *Initial connection*, *SSL*, *Waiting (TTFB)* and *Content Download*. This is the single most useful panel for answering \"why is this slow?\". A long **Waiting** time means the server took a while to think; a long **Content Download** means the file is big or the connection is slow.\n\nNow filter the list by **JS** and **CSS**. Count the requests. On an unoptimised page you may see sixty; each one is a trip through the machinery above. That count is why bundlers exist.\n\nFinally, open the **Performance** panel, record a reload, and look for the **FCP** (First Contentful Paint) and **LCP** (Largest Contentful Paint) markers. Everything you read in step 6 is visible there as a timeline of parse, style, layout and paint work.\n\nDoing this once on a site you use every day teaches more than reading about it ten times, which is why it is the first exercise.",
